      "text": "To evaluate \\(\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3})\\), we start by expressing the argument \\(27\\sqrt{3}\\) in terms of powers of 3.\n\nFirst, we rewrite \\(27\\) and \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) as expressions involving 3:\n\\[27 = 3^3\\]\n\\[\\sqrt{3} = 3^{1/2}\\]\n\nNow, we substitute these into the expression \\(27\\sqrt{3}\\):\n\\[27\\sqrt{3} = 3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2}\\]\n\nNext, we use the property of exponents that states \\(a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}\\) to combine the exponents:\n\\[3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2} = 3^{3 + 1/2} = 3^{6/2 + 1/2} = 3^{7/2}\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\log_3 (3^{7/2})\\). By the definition of logarithms, \\(\\log_b (b^x) = x\\), we have:\n\\[\\log_3 (3^{7/2}) = \\frac{7}{2}\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3})\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\\).",
