      "text": "To evaluate \\(\\log_3 27\\sqrt{3}\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Rewrite the expression inside the logarithm in terms of powers of 3:**\n   \\[\n   27\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n   First, express 27 and \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) as powers of 3:\n   \\[\n   27 = 3^3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sqrt{3} = 3^{1/2}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   27\\sqrt{3} = 3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Combine the exponents:**\n   When multiplying powers with the same base, add the exponents:\n   \\[\n   3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2} = 3^{3 + 1/2}\n   \\]\n   Convert 3 to a fraction with a common denominator:\n   \\[\n   3 = \\frac{6}{2}\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   3^{3 + 1/2} = 3^{6/2 + 1/2} = 3^{7/2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Write the original logarithm in terms of the combined exponent:**\n   \\[\n   \\log_3 27\\sqrt{3} = \\log_3 (3^{7/2})\n   \\]\n\n4. **Use the logarithm power rule:**\n   The power rule of logarithms states that \\(\\log_b (a^c) = c \\log_b a\\). Applying this rule:\n   \\[\n   \\log_3 (3^{7/2}) = \\frac{7}{2} \\log_3 3\n   \\]\n\n5. **Evaluate \\(\\log_3 3\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\log_3 3 = 1\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{7}{2} \\log_3 3 = \\frac{7}{2} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{7}{2}\n   \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\n\\]",
