      "text": "Given the problem $\\log_3 27\\sqrt3$, we first convert it into a base 3 logarithm:\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 [27\\sqrt3] = \\log_3 [3^3 \\cdot 3^{\\frac{3}{2}}].\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we combine the exponents since the bases are the same:\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 [3^3 \\cdot 3^{\\frac{3}{2}}] = \\log_3 [3^3 \\cdot 3^{\\frac{3}{2}}] = \\log_3 [3^{3 + \\frac{3}{2}}].\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the exponent inside the logarithm:\n\n\\[\n3 + \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} + \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{9}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 [3^{\\frac{9}{2}}].\n\\]\n\nBy the fundamental property of logarithms, we know that:\n\n\\[\n\\log_b (b^x) = x.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite $\\log_3 [3^{\\frac{9}{2}}]$ as:\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 [3^{\\frac{9}{2}}] = \\frac{9}{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{9}{2}}\n\\]\n\nTo verify the solution, we can use Python to compute the value of $\\log_3 [27\\sqrt3]$ and confirm the result is $\\frac{9}{2}$.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Given value\nvalue = 27 * (3 ** 0.5)\n\n# Calculate log base 3\nlog_value = math.log(value, 3)\nprint(log_value)\n```",
