      "text": "Given the expression $\\log_3 27\\sqrt3$, we start by expressing the product inside the logarithm in terms of powers of 3.\n\nFirst, we know that $27 = 3^3$ and $\\sqrt{3} = 3^{1/2}$. Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\\[\n27\\sqrt{3} = 3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2} = 3^{3 + 1/2} = 3^{7/2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to evaluate $\\log_3 3^{7/2}$. Using the logarithm power rule, $\\log_b (a^c) = c \\log_b a$, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_3 3^{7/2} = \\frac{7}{2} \\log_3 3\n\\]\n\nSince $\\log_3 3 = 1$, it follows that:\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{2} \\log_3 3 = \\frac{7}{2} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of $\\log_3 27\\sqrt{3}$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\n\\]",
